\section{Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) and Octree Spatial Decomposition}
\label{sec:amr_octree}

\subsection{Overview}
The simulation employs a block-structured Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR)
hierarchy built on an octree spatial decomposition. Refinement is
triggered by local curvature, hydrodynamic gradients, or shock
indicators, enabling efficient multi-scale resolution of relativistic
flows and spacetime geometry.

Each refinement level $\ell$ contains a set of disjoint cubic blocks
$\mathcal{B}^{(\ell)}_k$ with uniform resolution $\Delta x_\ell$, where
\[
\Delta x_\ell = \frac{\Delta x_0}{2^\ell}, \qquad \ell = 0,1,\dots,L_{\max}.
\]

\subsection{Octree Structure}
The computational domain $\Omega$ is recursively subdivided into
octants. Each parent block $\mathcal{B}^{(\ell)}$ generates eight
children at level $\ell+1$ when refinement criteria are met:
\[
\mathcal{B}^{(\ell)} \longrightarrow 
\left\{
\mathcal{B}^{(\ell+1)}_1, \dots, \mathcal{B}^{(\ell+1)}_8
\right\}.
\]

Each block stores:
\begin{itemize}
    \item cell-centered metric fields $(\tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \tilde{A}_{ij}, K, \phi)$,
    \item hydrodynamic conserved variables $(D, S_j, \tau)$,
    \item ghost zones for inter-block communication,
    \item refinement flags and error indicators.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Refinement Criteria}
Refinement is triggered when any of the following exceed user-defined
thresholds:
\begin{align}
\eta_{\mathrm{curv}} &= 
\left\| R_{ij} R^{ij} \right\|^{1/2}, \\
\eta_{\mathrm{shock}} &= 
\left\| \nabla P \right\|, \\
\eta_{\mathrm{hydro}} &= 
\left\| \nabla \rho \right\|.
\end{align}

A block is refined if
\[
\max\left(
\eta_{\mathrm{curv}},\,
\eta_{\mathrm{shock}},\,
\eta_{\mathrm{hydro}}
\right)
> \eta_{\mathrm{refine}},
\]
and derefined if all indicators fall below $\eta_{\mathrm{coarsen}}$.

\subsection{Prolongation and Restriction}
Field values are transferred between refinement levels using
high-order prolongation (interpolation) and restriction (averaging)
operators.

\paragraph{Prolongation (Fine Grid Interpolation).}
For a coarse-level field $u^{(\ell)}$, the fine-level values
$u^{(\ell+1)}$ are computed using 4th-order polynomial interpolation:
\[
u^{(\ell+1)}(x) = 
\sum_{k=-2}^{2} c_k\, u^{(\ell)}(x + k\Delta x_\ell),
\]
with coefficients $c_k$ chosen to preserve smoothness and avoid
spurious oscillations.

\paragraph{Restriction (Coarse Grid Averaging).}
Fine-level fields are volume-averaged to produce coarse-level values:
\[
u^{(\ell)} = 
\frac{1}{8}
\sum_{m=1}^{8} u^{(\ell+1)}_m.
\]

\subsection{Flux Correction Across Refinement Boundaries}
To ensure conservation across coarse–fine interfaces, fluxes are
synchronized using the Berger–Colella refluxing procedure:
\[
F^{(\ell)}_{\mathrm{corrected}} =
F^{(\ell)} +
\sum_{\mathrm{faces}}
\left(
F^{(\ell+1)}_{\mathrm{fine}} -
F^{(\ell)}_{\mathrm{coarse}}
\right).
\]

This guarantees global conservation of mass, momentum, and energy even
in the presence of strong shocks or rapidly evolving curvature.

\subsection{Time Integration and Subcycling}
Each refinement level evolves with its own timestep:
\[
\Delta t_\ell = \frac{\Delta t_0}{2^\ell},
\]
subject to the relativistic Courant condition:
\[
\Delta t_\ell \le 
\lambda_{\mathrm{CFL}}
\frac{\Delta x_\ell}{c}.
\]

Fine levels perform two substeps for each coarse-level step. After each
coarse step, synchronization ensures consistency of metric and fluid
fields across all levels.

\subsection{Parallel Decomposition and Load Balancing}
Blocks are distributed across MPI ranks using a Hilbert
space-filling curve to preserve spatial locality. Load balancing is
performed by minimizing the cost function
\[
C = \sum_{\ell} w_\ell\, N^{(\ell)}_{\mathrm{blocks}},
\]
where $w_\ell$ weights computational cost at refinement level $\ell$.

GPU kernels operate on block-local data, enabling efficient overlap of
communication and computation.

\subsection{AMR Verification Summary}
The AMR octree system preserves:
\begin{itemize}
    \item 4th-order convergence in smooth regions,
    \item conservative flux synchronization across refinement boundaries,
    \item stable gauge evolution under dynamic lapse collapse,
    \item shock resolution within $\le 2$ fine-grid cells,
    \item consistent multi-level time integration via subcycling.
\end{itemize}

This AMR framework enables efficient multi-scale resolution of
relativistic shocks, curvature gradients, and quantum-scale
perturbations within a unified simulation engine.